A map from a product of complete lattices into a complete lattice preserves directed suprema if and only if every slice in each variable does. This is Scott's Proposition 2.6. Continuity is read as preservation of directed suprema, two variables suffice, and the product carries the componentwise complete-lattice structure, whose induced topology is the product topology.

If the joint map preserves directed suprema, the image of a nonempty directed set under either slice embedding is nonempty and directed. The supremum of that image is the sliced pair, because one projection of the image is the original set and the other is a singleton. Joint preservation therefore restricts to each slice. Conversely, separate preservation gives monotone slices and hence a monotone joint map. For a nonempty directed set in the product, both projections are nonempty and directed, and the supremum is the pair of their suprema. Preservation in the first variable, then in the second, expresses the value at that pair as a supremum of values f(x, y). Directedness supplies a common upper bound in the set for witnesses of x and of y, and monotonicity places f(x, y) below the image of that bound, hence below the supremum of the image. The opposite inequality is monotonicity at members of the set.
